# ================= 3 stars =================
q(3,'🟢','heapq_layout',"Python heapq basics and the max-heap trick. `heapq` is a binary min-heap stored in a plain list. Given a list `vals` of integers (0 <= len <= 50), return `[pushed, heapified, three_pops, top_three_desc]` where `pushed` is the list you get after calling `heapq.heappush` for each value in order on an empty list, `heapified` is the list `heapq.heapify` produces from a copy of `vals`, `three_pops` is the first (up to) three values `heappop` returns from the `pushed` heap, and `top_three_desc` is the (up to) three largest values in descending order obtained from a max-heap built by pushing negated values. The two layouts are exactly what CPython 3.13's heapq produces. Also state the parent and child index formulas, the cost of heappush, heappop, heapify and peeking `h[0]`, and why the list is not sorted.",
  [{'vals':[5,3,8,1,9,2]},{'vals':[4,4,1]},{'vals':[]},{'vals':[9,8,7,6,5,4,3,2,1]}],'HP ED')
q(3,'🟢','kth_stream',"Kth largest element in a stream. Build a class `KthLargest(k, nums)` with `add(val)` that adds a value and returns the k-th largest value among all values seen so far (counting duplicates; the 1st largest is the maximum). Constraints: 1 <= k <= 10^4, 0 <= len(nums) <= 10^4, up to 10^4 calls to add, and len(nums) + 1 >= k so an answer always exists after the first add. Keep a min-heap of size k: say why its root is the answer, what each add costs (O(log k)), and why a max-heap of everything would be the wrong tool. The example gives the constructor arguments and the list of added values, and the expected output is the list of values add returns.",
  [{'k':3,'nums':[4,5,8,2],'adds':[3,5,10,9,4]},{'k':1,'nums':[],'adds':[-3,-2,-4,0]},{'k':2,'nums':[0],'adds':[-1,1,-2,-4,3]}],'HP ED')
q(3,'🟢','meeting_rooms1',f"Can one person attend all meetings. {IVN} Given `intervals` (0 <= len <= 10^4, 0 <= start < end <= 10^6), return True when no two meetings overlap. Sort by start time and compare neighbours; say why comparing only neighbours is enough after sorting, what the cost is, and why the pairwise O(n^2) check is the brute force.",
  [{'intervals':[[0,30],[5,10],[15,20]]},{'intervals':[[7,10],[2,4]]},{'intervals':[[1,5],[5,8]]},{'intervals':[]}],'IV ED')
q(3,'🟡','kth_largest',"Kth largest element in an array. Given integers `nums` (1 <= len <= 10^5, values in [-10^9, 10^9], duplicates allowed) and 1 <= k <= len, return the k-th largest value in sorted order, so in [3, 2, 2, 1] the 2nd and 3rd largest are both 2. Give three solutions and compare them: sort (O(n log n)), a min-heap of size k (O(n log k) time, O(k) space) and quickselect (expected O(n), worst O(n^2) with a bad pivot, and it rearranges the input). Say when each one wins: k small and data streaming, k close to n, input may be modified, worst-case guarantee needed.",
  [{'nums':[3,2,1,5,6,4],'k':2},{'nums':[3,2,3,1,2,4,5,5,6],'k':4},{'nums':[7],'k':1},{'nums':[2,2,2],'k':3}],'HP CK ED')
q(3,'🟡','merge_intervals',f"Merge intervals. This question uses CLOSED intervals [start, end] with start <= end, so [1, 4] and [4, 5] touch at 4 and must be merged. Given an unsorted list `intervals` (1 <= len <= 10^4, 0 <= start <= end <= 10^6), return the minimal list of disjoint closed intervals covering exactly the same points, sorted by start. Sort by start, then fold each interval into the last merged one when its start is <= the last end, taking the max of the two ends (explain why a contained interval must not shrink the end). State the complexity (O(n log n), dominated by the sort).",
  [{'intervals':[[1,3],[2,6],[8,10],[15,18]]},{'intervals':[[1,4],[4,5]]},{'intervals':[[1,4],[0,4]]},{'intervals':[[1,10],[2,3],[4,5]]},{'intervals':[[3,3]]}],'IV CK ED')
q(3,'🟡','insert_interval',"Insert interval. This question uses CLOSED intervals [start, end] with start <= end, where touching intervals count as overlapping. `intervals` is already sorted by start and pairwise disjoint (0 <= len <= 10^4), and `new` is one more interval. Return the sorted disjoint list after inserting `new` and merging everything it overlaps or touches. Do it in one O(n) pass in three phases (copy the intervals that end before `new` starts, merge the overlapping ones into `new`, copy the rest) without re-sorting, and say why the binary-search variant still costs O(n) in a Python list.",
  [{'intervals':[[1,3],[6,9]],'new':[2,5]},{'intervals':[[1,2],[3,5],[6,7],[8,10],[12,16]],'new':[4,8]},{'intervals':[],'new':[5,7]},{'intervals':[[1,5]],'new':[6,8]},{'intervals':[[1,5]],'new':[5,6]}],'IV ED')
q(3,'🟡','meeting_rooms2',f"Meeting rooms II (minimum number of rooms). {IVN} Given `intervals` (0 <= len <= 10^5, 0 <= start < end <= 10^9), return the minimum number of rooms needed so that no two overlapping meetings share a room (0 when there are no meetings). Solve it with a min-heap of end times after sorting by start (reuse a room when the earliest end is <= the new start, which is the half-open rule), then with a sweep over sorted +1/-1 events, and say which tie order at equal times makes the sweep correct.",
  [{'intervals':[[0,30],[5,10],[15,20]]},{'intervals':[[7,10],[2,4]]},{'intervals':[[1,5],[5,8],[8,9]]},{'intervals':[[1,10],[2,3],[3,4],[4,5]]},{'intervals':[]}],'HP IV CK ED')
q(3,'🟡','top_k_words',"Top K frequent words. Given a list of lowercase `words` (1 <= len <= 10^5) and an integer k (1 <= k <= number of distinct words), return the k most frequent words ordered by frequency descending, and alphabetically ascending among words with equal frequency. The answer is unique under this rule. Use a Counter and a heap (not a full sort) and show the tie-breaking trick: push (-count, word) so the tuple ordering yields count descending and word ascending in one min-heap, which works because the word part is only compared when counts tie. Compare with the integer version in Part 1 Q10 (there any order is accepted and bucket counting gives O(n)); say why bucket counting does not remove the tie-break work here.",
  [{'words':['i','love','leetcode','i','love','coding'],'k':2},{'words':['the','day','is','sunny','the','the','the','sunny','is','is'],'k':4},{'words':['a','b','c'],'k':3}],'HP ED')
q(3,'🟡','k_closest',"K closest points to the origin. Given `points` as [x, y] integer pairs (1 <= len <= 10^5, |x|, |y| <= 10^4, duplicates allowed) and 1 <= k <= len, return k points with the smallest Euclidean distance to (0, 0). Compare squared distances, never take square roots. The checker accepts any k points whose squared distances are the k smallest (as a multiset), in any order, so when several points tie at the k-th distance any of them may be returned; the examples show one accepted answer. Give a max-heap of size k (O(n log k)) and a quickselect on squared distance (expected O(n)), and say why heapq.nsmallest with a key is the short answer and when it is not (large k).",
  [{'points':[[1,3],[-2,2]],'k':1},{'points':[[3,3],[5,-1],[-2,4]],'k':2},{'points':[[1,1],[-1,-1],[2,2],[0,3]],'k':2}],'HP ED')
q(3,'🟡','merge_k_arrays',"Merge k sorted arrays with a heap. `lists` holds k sorted (non-decreasing) integer lists, 0 <= k <= 100, each of length 0 to 500, total length N <= 10^4 (the same list length bound as the linked-list version in Part 6 Q12, which uses the heap only as a merge tool; here the heap is the subject). Return one sorted list. Keep a heap of (value, list index, position) so the heap never holds more than k entries and ties never compare anything but integers; each pop is followed by at most one push. State the cost (O(N log k) time, O(k) extra heap space), compare with concatenating and sorting (O(N log N)) and with pairwise divide and conquer merging (also O(N log k)), and say why `heapq.merge` is the library version and what it returns (an iterator).",
  [{'lists':[[1,4,5],[1,3,4],[2,6]]},{'lists':[[],[1],[]]},{'lists':[]},{'lists':[[2,2],[2],[1,2,3]]}],'HP CK ED')
q(3,'🟡','task_scheduler',"Task scheduler with cooldown. `tasks` is a string of uppercase letters (1 <= len <= 10^4), one task per letter, each taking one time slot; a CPU runs one task or idles per slot, and two runs of the same letter must have at least n other slots (idle or other tasks) between them (0 <= n <= 100). Return the minimum number of slots to finish all tasks, in any order. Solve it with a max-heap of remaining counts plus a cooldown queue, then with the closed form max(len(tasks), (max_count - 1) * (n + 1) + number_of_letters_with_max_count), and explain why the formula is a lower bound that the schedule always meets.",
  [{'tasks':'AAABBB','n':2},{'tasks':'AAABBB','n':0},{'tasks':'AAAAAABCDEFG','n':2},{'tasks':'AABC','n':1},{'tasks':'A','n':50}],'HP GR ED')
q(3,'🟡','jump_game1',"Jump game (can you reach the end). `nums` holds non-negative integers (1 <= len <= 10^4), where nums[i] is the maximum jump length from index i (any length from 0 to nums[i] is allowed). Starting at index 0, return True if you can reach the last index. Give the O(n) greedy that tracks the farthest reachable index and returns False when the scan reaches an index beyond it, state the invariant, and say why the DP and the BFS versions are O(n^2) in the worst case.",
  [{'nums':[2,3,1,1,4]},{'nums':[3,2,1,0,4]},{'nums':[0]},{'nums':[1,0,1]}],'GR ED')
q(3,'🟡','jump_game2',"Jump game II (minimum jumps). Same moves as the reachability version: nums[i] is the maximum jump length from index i, 1 <= len <= 10^4, and the last index is guaranteed reachable. Return the minimum number of jumps from index 0 to the last index (0 when len is 1). Give the O(n) greedy that treats the reachable indices as BFS levels (current level end, farthest next reach, count a jump when the scan hits the level end), and say why this is BFS without a queue and why the scan stops before the last index.",
  [{'nums':[2,3,1,1,4]},{'nums':[2,3,0,1,4]},{'nums':[0]},{'nums':[1,1,1,1]}],'GR ED')
q(3,'🟡','gas_station',"Gas station on a circle. There are n stations in a circle (1 <= n <= 10^5); `gas[i]` is the fuel at station i and `cost[i]` the fuel needed to drive from i to i+1 (mod n); the tank is empty at the start and unlimited. Return the smallest starting index from which you can complete one full lap clockwise (arriving at a station with exactly 0 fuel is fine), or -1 if no start works. Give the O(n) one-pass greedy (if total gas < total cost return -1; otherwise reset the candidate to i + 1 whenever the running tank goes negative) and prove it with the two facts: a failure at j from start s means every station between s and j also fails, and total gas >= total cost implies a start exists. Note that some formulations promise a unique answer; for general input it is not unique (for example gas = [1, 1], cost = [1, 1] works from both stations), which is why this prompt asks for the smallest index.",
  [{'gas':[1,2,3,4,5],'cost':[3,4,5,1,2]},{'gas':[2,3,4],'cost':[3,4,3]},{'gas':[1,1],'cost':[1,1]},{'gas':[5],'cost':[4]}],'GR ED')
q(3,'🟡','reorganize',"Reorganize string. Given a string `s` of lowercase letters (1 <= len <= 500), rearrange its characters so that no two adjacent characters are equal, and return the result, or the empty string if that is impossible. Several answers can be valid, and the checker accepts any rearrangement that is a permutation of `s` with no equal neighbours (and requires '' exactly when none exists); the examples show one accepted answer. Give the max-heap solution (pop the most frequent letter, hold it back one step) and the closed condition for feasibility, max_count <= (len + 1) // 2, with a short proof of necessity (an arrangement of length L has at most ceil(L / 2) slots for any one letter).",
  [{'s':'aab'},{'s':'aaab'},{'s':'vvvlo'},{'s':'a'},{'s':'aabbcc'}],'HP GR ED')
q(3,'🟡','non_overlap',"Non-overlapping intervals (fewest removals). Intervals are half-open [start, end) with start < end, so [1, 2) and [2, 3) do not overlap. Given `intervals` (1 <= len <= 10^5, values in [-5*10^4, 5*10^4]), return the minimum number of intervals to remove so that the rest are pairwise non-overlapping. Sort by END time and keep an interval whenever its start is >= the last kept end (the earliest-finish greedy); give the exchange argument in two lines, show a counter-example for sorting by start time, and say why the answer equals len minus the activity-selection maximum.",
  [{'intervals':[[1,2],[2,3],[3,4],[1,3]]},{'intervals':[[1,2],[1,2],[1,2]]},{'intervals':[[1,2],[2,3]]},{'intervals':[[1,100],[11,22],[1,11],[2,12]]}],'GR IV ED')
q(3,'🔴','median_stream',"Median of a data stream. Maintain a running median: process `adds` (1 <= len <= 10^5, integers in [-10^6, 10^6]) one value at a time and return the list of medians after each insertion as floats (the middle value when the count is odd, the mean of the two middle values when it is even, so [1, 2] gives 1.5). Use two heaps, a max-heap `lo` (negated values) for the smaller half and a min-heap `hi` for the larger half, keep the invariant len(lo) == len(hi) or len(lo) == len(hi) + 1, and describe the push-then-rebalance step that keeps every element of lo <= every element of hi. State the cost (O(log n) per add, O(1) per median query) and compare with keeping a sorted list and using bisect.insort (O(n) per insert because of the memmove, but fast for small n).",
  [{'adds':[1,2,3]},{'adds':[5,15,1,3]},{'adds':[-1,-2,-3,-4,-5]},{'adds':[2,2,2,2]},{'adds':[7]}],'HP ED')
