q(2,'🟢','last_stone',"Last stone weight. `stones` holds positive integers (1 <= len <= 30, each <= 1000). Repeatedly take the two heaviest stones; if they are equal both are destroyed, otherwise the lighter is destroyed and the heavier becomes the difference. Return the weight of the last stone, or 0 if none is left. Show the max-heap via negation (heapq has no max-heap), say what happens with a single stone, and state the cost (O(n log n)).",
  [{'stones':[2,7,4,1,8,1]},{'stones':[1]},{'stones':[3,3]},{'stones':[10,4,2,10]}],'HP ED')
q(2,'🟢','cookies',"Assign cookies. `g[i]` is the minimum cookie size child i will accept and `s[j]` is the size of cookie j (0 <= len(g), len(s) <= 3*10^4, sizes in [1, 2^31 - 1]). Each child gets at most one cookie and each cookie goes to at most one child; a child is content when the cookie size is >= g[i]. Return the maximum number of content children. Sort both lists and walk the cookies with a pointer over the children (smallest cookie that satisfies the least greedy unmatched child), and give the exchange argument for why using the smallest sufficient cookie never hurts.",
  [{'g':[1,2,3],'s':[1,1]},{'g':[1,2],'s':[1,2,3]},{'g':[],'s':[4]},{'g':[5,6],'s':[1,2]}],'GR ED')
